3-634d-5917-86bf-fd5c5bf44384" 13194139534530 17592186045417 true] [17592186424371 1075 #edb/ref 17592186424339 13194139534530 17592186045417 true] [17592186424371 1075 #edb/ref 17592186424344 13194139534530 17592186045417 true] [17592186424371 1075 #edb/ref 17592186424355 13194139534530 17592186045417 true] [17592186424371 1075 #edb/ref 17592186424358 13194139534530 17592186045417 true] [17592186424371 1075 #edb/ref 17592186424365 13194139534530 17592186045417 true] [17592186424371 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 13194139534530 17592186045417 true] [17592186424371 1077 "selection" 13194139534530 17592186045417 true] [17592186424371 1078 #edb/ref 17592186424344 13194139534530 17592186045417 true] [17592186424371 1079 #uuid "c9a04253-21f8-57fa-9da2-eb6b04eff7f1" 13194139534530 17592186045417 true] [17592186424372 1075 #edb/ref 17592186424333 13194139534530 17592186045417 true] [17592186424372 1075 #edb/ref 17592186424335 13194139534530 17592186045417 true] [17592186424372 1075 #edb/ref 17592186424342 13194139534530 17592186045417 true] [17592186424372 1075 #edb/ref 17592186424359 13194139534530 17592186045417 true] [17592186424372 1075 #edb/ref 17592186424366 13194139534530 17592186045417 true] [17592186424372 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 13194139534530 17592186045417 true] [17592186424372 1077 "selection" 13194139534530 17592186045417 true] [17592186424372 1078 #edb/ref 17592186424335 13194139534530 17592186045417 true] [17592186424372 1079 #uuid "d8df995d-74a1-505c-a3a5-e60115444fb0" 13194139534530 17592186045417 true] [17592186424373 1075 #edb/ref 17592186424348 13194139534530 17592186045417 true] [17592186424373 1075 #edb/ref 17592186424351 13194139534530 17592186045417 true] [17592186424373 1075 #edb/ref 17592186424352 13194139534530 17592186045417 true] [17592186424373 1075 #edb/ref 17592186424360 13194139534530 17592186045417 true] [17592186424373 1075 #edb/ref 17592186424361 13194139534530 17592186045417 true] [17592186424373 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 13194139534530 17592186045417 true] [17592186424373 1077 "selection" 13194139534530 17592186045417 true] [17592186424373 1078 #edb/ref 17592186424351 13194139534530 17592186045417 true] [17592186424373 1079 #uuid "e77545cc-1b3a-5e85-b752-ea1b743f1c43" 13194139534530 17592186045417 true] [17592186424374 1068 #edb/ref 17592186424371 13194139534530 17592186045417 true] [17592186424374 1069 "q-97647" 13194139534530 17592186045417 true] [17592186424374 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139534530 17592186045417 true] [17592186424374 1071 "A child is playing with a tablet. The apps on the tablet are labeled with numbers $4$ through $21.$ If the child chooses an app at random, what is the probability that the app number they pick is more than $16?$" 13194139534530 17592186045417 true] [17592186424374 1072 #uuid "1f759cee-8f8e-57dc-b752-40d429fccaa2" 13194139534530 17592186045417 true] [17592186424374 1074 "Let $X$ represent the number of the app picked. From the problem statement, we know that $X∼U\\{4,5,…,21\\},$ and we wish to compute $P(X>16).$\n\nIn general, if $X$ follows a discrete uniform distribution over a set $S,$ then $X$ has the following probability mass function:\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{|S|}, & x\\in S \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHere, we have $X∼U\\{4,5,…,21\\},$ and the set $S=\\{4,5,…,21\\}$ has $|S|=21-4+1=18$ items. So, we have\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{18}, & x=4,5,…,21 \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn $S,$ the items that satisfy $X>16$ are $17,18,19,20,21.$ There are $5$ of these items. Therefore,\n\n$$\nP(X>16)=5\\cdot \\frac{1}{18}=\\frac{5}{18}.\n$$" 13194139534530 17592186045417 true] [17592186424375 1068 #edb/ref 17592186424367 13194139534530 17592186045417 true] [17592186424375 1069 "q-97754" 13194139534530 17592186045417 true] [17592186424375 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139534530 17592186045417 true] [17592186424375 1071 "Which of the following random variables can be modeled as a discrete uniform distribution?\n\nI. The temperature of a town on a certain day.\n\nII. The number of letters of a word randomly selected from an English dictionary.\n\nIII. The number of typos in a randomly selected book." 13194139534530 17592186045417 true] [17592186424375 1072 #uuid "239e34ce-3c73-5bae-b143-3d41bff8bf51" 13194139534530 17592186045417 true] [17592186424375 1074 "We can use the discrete uniform distribution to model the outcome of a single event when the support $S$ is finite, and each $x\\in S$ is equally likely.\n\nWith that in mind, let's consider each of the given random variables.\n\n- Random variable I cannot be modeled as a discrete uniform distribution. This is because a temperature is a real number, so the support is not finite.\n\n- Random variable II cannot be modeled as a discrete uniform distribution. Each outcome is not equally likely (there are more short words than very long words, for example).\n\n- Random variable III cannot be modeled as a discrete uniform distribution. This is because the number of typos can be any positive integer. Moreover, each outcome is not equally likely.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"None.\"" 13194139534530 17592186045417 true] [17592186424376 1068 #edb/ref 17592186424373 13194139534530 17592186045417 true] [17592186424376 1069 "q-97660" 13194139534530 17592186045417 true] [17592186424376 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139534530 17592186045417 true] [17592186424376 1071 "Jack wants to buy a single issue of a particular comic series with $80$ issues. Unfortunately, the comic store has neither the first $3$ issues nor the final issue in stock. If Jack buys a comic at random from the available issues, what is the probability that he chooses either issue $20$ or issue $23?$" 13194139534530 17592186045417 true] [17592186424376 1072 #uuid "574dc243-2e55-5ce7-9e9d-a12e501cd5c3" 13194139534530 17592186045417 true] [17592186424376 1074 "Let $X$ represent the issue Jack selects. From the problem statement, we know that $X∼U\\{4,5,…,79\\},$ and we wish to compute $P(X=20\\,\\,or\\,\\,X=23).$\n\nIn general, if $X$ follows a discrete uniform distribution over a set $S,$ then $X$ has the following probability mass function:\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{|S|}, & x\\in S \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHere, we have $X∼U\\{4,5,…,79\\},$ and the set $S=\\{4,5,…,79\\}$ has $|S|=79-4+1=76$ items. So, we have\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{76}, & x=4,5,…,79 \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn $S,$ the items that satisfy the given condition are $20,23.$ There are $2$ of these elements. Therefore,\n\n$$\nP(X=20\\,\\,or\\,\\,X=23)=2\\cdot \\frac{1}{76}=\\frac{1}{38}.\n$$" 13194139534530 17592186045417 true] [17592186424377 1068 #edb/ref 17592186424369 13194139534530 17592186045417 true] [17592186424377 1069 "q-148394" 13194139534530 17592186045417 true] [17592186424377 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139534530 17592186045417 true] [17592186424377 1071 "Which of the following random variables can be modeled as a discrete uniform distribution?\n\nI. The number obtained when picking an integer between $0$ and $10$ at random.\n\nII. The number obtained when picking a real number between $5$ and $9$ at random.\n\nIII. The number of clients arriving at a shopping mall in a day." 13194139534530 17592186045417 true] [17592186424377 1072 #uuid "78d51529-5603-5294-836c-698a99289cb8" 13194139534530 17592186045417 true] [17592186424377 1074 "We can use the discrete uniform distribution to model the outcome of a single event when the support $S$ is finite, and each $x\\in S$ is equally likely.\n\nWith that in mind, let's consider each of the given random variables.\n\n- Random variable I can be modeled as a discrete uniform distribution. This is because there are a finite number of integers between $0$ and $10,$ and since the selection is random, each integer has an equal probability of being selected.\n\n- Random variable II cannot be modeled as a discrete uniform distribution. This is because there are infinitely many real numbers between $5$ and $9,$ and so the support is not finite.\n\n- Random variable III cannot be modeled as a discrete uniform distribution. This is because the number of clients arriving at a shopping mall can be any positive integer, and each outcome is not equally likely.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\"" 13194139534530 17592186045417 true] [17592186424378 1068 #edb/ref 17592186424368 13194139534530 17592186045417 true] [17592186424378 1069 "q-148408" 13194139534530 17592186045417 true] [17592186424378 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139534530 17592186045417 true] [17592186424378 1071 "When rolling a fair six-sided die, what is the probability of getting a prime number?" 13194139534530 17592186045417 true] [17592186424378 1072 #uuid "a490336d-2a2e-5374-a15f-1401e77380a0" 13194139534530 17592186045417 true] [17592186424378 1074 "Let $X$ represent the number on which the die lands. From the problem statement, we know that $X∼U\\{1,2,…,6\\},$ and we wish to compute $P(X\\in \\{2,3,5\\}).$\n\nIn general, if $X$ follows a discrete uniform distribution over a set $S,$ then $X$ has the following probability mass function:\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{|S|}, & x\\in S \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHere, we have $X∼U\\{1,2,…,6\\},$ and the set $S=\\{1,2,…,6\\}$ has $|S|=6$ items. So, we have\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{6}, & x=1,2,…,6 \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn $S,$ the items that satisfy $X\\in \\{2,3,5\\}$ are $2,3,5.$ There are $3$ of these elements. Therefore,\n\n$$\nP(X\\in \\{2,3,5\\})=3\\cdot \\frac{1}{6}=\\frac{1}{2}.\n$$" 13194139534530 17592186045417 true] [17592186424379 1068 #edb/ref 17592186424372 13194139534530 17592186045417 true] [17592186424379 1069 "q-148406" 13194139534530 17592186045417 true] [17592186424379 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139534530 17592186045417 true] [17592186424379 1071 "The only available spaces in a multi-story parking lot are on the second floor, numbered from $215$ to $268.$\nIf Emma parks in one of the spaces at random, what is the probability that she parks in a space which is numbered between $234$ and $245$ inclusive?" 13194139534530 17592186045417 true] [17592186424379 1072 #uuid "e66fdc5b-c55e-53fe-9d08-47c56ef73570" 13194139534530 17592186045417 true] [17592186424379 1074 "Let $X$ represent the parking space Emma picks. From the problem statement, we know that $X∼U\\{215,216,…,268\\},$ and we wish to compute $P(234\\le X\\le 245).$\n\nIn general, if $X$ follows a discrete uniform distribution over a set $S,$ then $X$ has the following probability mass function:\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{|S|}, & x\\in S \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHere, we have $X∼U\\{215,216,…,268\\},$ and the set $S=\\{215,216,…,268\\}$ has $|S|=268-215+1=54$ items. So, we have\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{54}, & x=215,216,…,268 \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn $S,$ the items that satisfy $234\\le X\\le 245$ are $234,235,…,245.$ There are $245-234+1=12$ of these elements. Therefore,\n\n$$\nP(234\\le X\\le 245)=12\\cdot \\frac{1}{54}=\\frac{2}{9}.\n$$" 13194139534530 17592186045417 true] [17592186424380 1068 #edb/ref 17592186424370 13194139534530 17592186045417 true] [17592186424380 1069 "q-97753" 13194139534530 17592186045417 true] [17592186424380 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139534530 17592186045417 true] [17592186424380 1071 "Which of the following random variables can be modeled as a discrete uniform distribution?\n\nI. The number obtained when picking a card from a set of cards numbered from $1$ to $12,$ at random.\n\nII. The number obtained when throwing a standard die that is biased toward landing on $1.$\n\nIII. The color obtained when a ball from a bag containing $5$ balls with distinct colors is chosen randomly." 13194139534530 17592186045417 true] [17592186424380 1072 #uuid "fc3edf0b-9469-5401-acc3-594a44dac66e" 13194139534530 17592186045417 true] [17592186424380 1074 "We can use the discrete uniform distribution to model the outcome of a single event when the support $S$ is finite, and each $x\\in S$ is equally likely.\n\nWith that in mind, let's consider each of the given random variables.\n\n- Random variable I can be modeled as a discrete uniform distribution. There are a finite number of cards, and since the selection is made at random, each card has an equal probability of being selected.\n\n- Random variable II cannot be modeled as a discrete uniform distribution. Since the die is not fair, each outcome is not equally likely, because there is a higher probability of getting $1.$\n\n- Random variable III can be modeled as a discrete uniform distribution. This is because there are a finite number of distinct colors, and since the selection is made at random, each color has an equal probability of being picked.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and III only.\"" 13194139534530 17592186045417 true]] :edb/tempids {"answer-0e218fff-12f9-5fa5-922d-87fcb0d3466e" 17592186424332 "answer-0e5e3c03-d706-5575-856d-866eb3d9500c" 17592186424333 "answer-15086c87-9c13-579c-ba6e-748eaf611b0c" 17592186424334 "answer-3c08f617-d8ee-596f-b133-64ab4350a77c" 17592186424335 "answer-3e3e30ef-68e6-519f-85f4-fee8d10f81e3" 17592186424336 "answer-46101349-6d6a-5bb0-9207-0aaec4554d62" 17592186424337 "answer-49dca3bb-2186-5f51-be14-255602c25375" 17592186424338 "answer-5547a59d-4082-5471-8b71-d59a07d32d36" 17592186424339 "answer-59a9fa70-556c-52b8-b78c-ec478f8ef8d4" 17592186424340 "answer-61ef3bcd-05c5-505d-b1b7-15232aa92580" 17592186424341 "answer-67067611-aa63-5326-8173-b77d14f9f6aa" 17592186424342 "answer-69e75bc6-66fd-55e9-bf42-99525bc872c0" 17592186424343 "answer-6b0f2fd4-cce9-5b1f-a0fc-bbb4de04e32c" 17592186424344 "answer-6d59c617-fb6d-55c2-8065-4b569c87d39a" 17592186424345 "answer-6ec6426d-a527-5edf-abdc-752ce809d846" 175921864